<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="salicylate glucosides biosynthesis IV" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6762" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-110 &quot;Salicylic acid&quot;| (salicylate; SA) is an aromatic compound commonly found throughout the plant kingdom that is also found in some bacteria. In plants |FRAME: CPD-110| affects numerous biological and physiological processes, including plant defense (via the systematic acquired resistance system |CITS: [15283665][12873532]|), allelopathy, flowering, and stomatal movement |CITS: [SCHULZ93]|. Given its important signaling role, plants often chemically modify |FRAME: CPD-110| by attaching glycosyl, methyl, or other groups to |FRAME: CPD-110| to alters its activity, volatility, or other properties |CITS [PIERPOINT94][SCHULZ93]|. In particular, |FRAME: CPD-12628| (SAG) is believed to be more water-soluble than |FRAME: CPD-110|, thus enabling it enter the vacuole more easily via an ABC transporter or a H&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;-antiporter |CITS: [15032822][15871031]|. This process may serve to prevent plants from being over-exposed to active SA and/or may enable the storage of this important signaling compound |CITS: [18248508]|. Notably, in some species, enzymes that perform this glucosylation are up-regulated as levels of |FRAME: CPD-110| rise |CITS: [SCHULZ93]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; A number of plant species have been shown to catalyze the formation of |FRAME: CPD-12628| (SAG), such as |FRAME: TAX-4097 (tobacco)&quot;| |CITS: [15871031]|, |FRAME: TAX-3689| (poplar) species and hybrids, |CITS: [20177744][17599371]|, |FRAME:TAX-3708| |CITS: [19449088]|, and |FRAME: TAX-4530| (rice) |CITS: [12228500]|. |FRAME: CPD-12628 SAG| may be further modified to produce additional salicylate glucosides that differ between species. For example, |FRAME:CPD-12663| is made by |FRAME: ORG-5993| (see |FRAME: PWY-6621|) |CITS: [18248508]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
