    <DataNode elementId="AT2G43840-MONOMER" textLabel="Flavonol 7-O-beta-glucosyltransferase UGT74F1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q3EBH1" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">UGT74F1 is a glucosyltransferase that acts on |FRAME: BENZOATE| and |FRAME: CPD-110| |CITS: [17085977][11641410]|. It also appears to act on |FRAME: ANTHRANILATE| |CITS: [12475971]|, |FRAME: CPD-520 quercetin||CITS: [15352060]|, and |FRAME: P-AMINO-BENZOATE| |CITS: [18385129]|. A closely related enzyme in Arabidopsis, |FRAME:AT2G43820-MONOMER UGT74F2| seems to possess many over-lapping activities, but, it appears to be expressed at a higher level, and does have some biochemical differences. For instance, while |FRAME:AT2G43820-MONOMER UGT74F2| can catalyze the formation of |FRAME: CPD-12629 SGE| and |FRAME: CPD-12628 SAG| &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;, UGT74F1 only appears to promote the formation of |FRAME: CPD-12628 SAG| |CITS: [18248508]|. There is &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; evidence for its participation in salicylic acid modification based on the reduction of |FRAME: CPD-12628 SAG| observed in a &lt;i&gt;ugt74f1&lt;/i&gt; mutant |CITS: [18248508]|. To date, the subcellular localization and precise biological function of UGT74F1 remain unknown, but, given its ability to act upon several different substrates, it may be involved in multiple biochemical pathways &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt;.</Comment>
